Package: geoconnexr
Title: Discover, Crosswalk, and Package Geoconnex Water Data
Version: 0.0.0.9000
Authors@R:
    person("Kyle", "Onda", , "konda@lincolninst.edu", role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"))
Description: Tools for discovering water-data sites and datasets through the
    Geoconnex persistent identifier, reference feature, and knowledge graph
    infrastructure. Provides typed identifier crosswalk foundations, safe
    query-template assets, versioned contracts, and reproducible-procedure
    metadata for watershed data snapshots. The package is in an architecture
    spike stage; its protocol slices provide bounded, cache-aware PID
    resolution, fail-closed JSON-LD retrieval and expansion, tolerant
    diagnostic-producing Geoconnex profile parsing, and a native bounded OGC
    API Features reference client with a validated provider-gage PID crosswalk
    checksum-pinned release-scoped COMID/mainstem crosswalks, and a bounded
    USGS NLDI HUC12 outlet crosswalk. Custom
    polygon AOIs have bounded offline CRS84 recipe construction and
    integrity-checked hydration boundaries, and a public evidence-only
    closed-tree verifier checks manifest-relative snapshot integrity offline
    without loading or replaying resources. A bounded internal catalog value
    object and creation-only catalog snapshot writer provide a deterministic
    offline packaging substrate; public catalog-only snapshot creation exposes
    that verified path while rejecting fetch, report, replay, Frictionless, and
    overwrite claims. A bounded public read-only accessor binds the
    deterministic request export to its authoritative manifest ledger and
    returns exact typed request evidence without parsing other catalog
    resources. A bounded internal canonical CSV loader admits the three fixed
    redacted catalog resources only as byte-rebound character tables without
    reconstructing discarded identities. A public read-only typed redacted
    view derives only fixed geometry, UTC, logical, and JSON-array fields while
    retaining the canonical character evidence and explicitly declining live
    catalog reconstruction or replay. An internal package-input value object
    now admits exact catalog, fetched, and harmonized stages; the latter two
    require an explicit catalog whose AOI and dataset identity rebind to the
    embedded fetch plan. It preserves native payloads but performs no
    publication, optional-format work, or replay. A subsequent internal
    bounded serializer derives deterministic in-memory catalog, fetch-index,
    native-result, and harmonized CSV/raw resources while preserving exact
    provider bytes only where the fetched contract retained them. An internal
    creation-only publisher writes that exact bundle and a deterministic
    manifest through verified sibling staging, atomically exposes only an
    absent destination, and verifies the exposed tree again. Public
    creation-only package writing now exposes this exact pipeline for catalog,
    fetched, and harmonized inputs; the latter two require their explicit
    source catalog, and only deterministic CSV/raw resources with retained
    provider bytes are admitted. A public byte-preserving loader verifies that
    fixed package profile before and after reading and returns exact bounded
    resource bytes without reconstructing workflow objects or authorizing
    replay. A public canonical table view parses verified package CSV bytes
    into character-only tibbles only when they round-trip exactly through the
    fixed quote-all UTF-8 profile; native raw resources remain opaque. An
    internal typed hydration substrate applies only fixed package-owned
    storage types, retains the complete canonical evidence, and leaves
    provider-native tables and raw payloads uninterpreted. A public typed
    inspection wrapper exposes that exact redacted, read-only result without
    reconstructing live workflow objects. Public verified replacement admits
    only fixed-writer destinations, stages both package generations through
    sibling paths, synchronously restores the prior package on detected
    failures, and returns evidence binding both verified generations. Optional
    package metadata for Arrow and Quarto can be inspected through a bounded
    internal preflight without loading either namespace. Arrow 14.0.0 is now
    the reviewed minimum for an internal fixed in-memory Parquet 2.4 serializer
    that rechecks required exports, redacts typed observations, and verifies an
    exact raw-byte read-back; the reviewed public Parquet profile carries those
    bytes through verified creation, loading, hydration, and replacement.
    An internal Quarto report boundary derives one fixed code-free summary from
    a typed package view, renders it only through a reviewed CLI with execution
    and caching disabled, verifies bounded minimal HTML without active or
    external content, and removes its private render tree. A report-aware
    profile binds the exact HTML to its originating bundle and carries it
    through verified staged creation and owned replacement. Explicit public
    report creation now retains an owned typed source package through final
    lineage verification and removes it afterward; public loading preserves
    HTML as exact opaque bytes, and a report accessor revalidates isolated HTML
    for in-memory access or absent-file export without invoking Quarto.
    Public offline replay inspection now verifies and loads only those fixed
    catalog-snapshot and package profiles as typed redacted stored state,
    including an existing report, while making no recipe-execution or live
    refresh claim. An internal deterministic Frictionless Data Package v1
    descriptor binds each fixed resource path, size, and digest in memory,
    declares exact all-string schemas for canonical CSV tables, and leaves
    non-CSV resources explicit and generic. A pinned Python CLI gate validates
    the fixed CSV package stages and real retained-raw, Arrow/Parquet, and
    verified-report bundles in continuous integration; non-CSV resources use
    an opaque-file transport profile that prevents semantic parser dispatch
    while retaining their true format in custom metadata. Public opt-in fixed
    packages now publish that descriptor as a manifest-bound resource,
    including packages with retained raw bodies, fixed Arrow/Parquet
    observations, or verified report HTML. Replacement and offline typed
    inspection preserve the combined evidence, and loading rederives the
    descriptor from verified stored bytes. Live refresh and procedural replay
    remain deferred until a complete request recipe exists. An internal
    public publisher profile now converts exact catalog site and dataset tables
    into deterministic local JSON-LD, validates candidate profiles with structured
    findings, and publishes one bounded XML sitemap through verified staging.
    Publisher operations do not fetch contexts, submit content remotely, or
    overwrite existing destinations. An installed language-neutral
    conformance corpus pins profile, finding, and sitemap known answers for R
    and a dependency-free Python feasibility harness. An internal
    selection-only fetch plan binds catalog distributions to strict handler
    metadata without constructing requests or executing providers. A separate
    internal host preflight uses bounded direct metadata reads for selected
    optional-package versions without loading namespaces and never claims
    execution readiness. A host-independent internal value object records
    deterministic, redacted, policy-bound direct-CSV GET intents without
    allocating budgets or authorizing transport. A second host-independent
    internal boundary allocates explicit global physical-attempt and byte
    reservations, holds non-CSV shares, and emits bounded non-executable
    direct-CSV logical request plans without transport or parser execution.
    An offline response boundary validates one bounded caller-supplied
    direct-CSV response candidate while explicitly withholding provider
    provenance, budget consumption, parsing, transport, and execution claims.
    A strict offline UTF-8 CSV boundary then parses only those exact retained
    bytes into a bounded character-only result contract without loading an
    optional parser package or upgrading the caller-supplied provenance claim.
    An internal single-attempt executor can now run one selected direct-CSV
    request through the DNS-pinned package transport, validate and parse the
    provider response, and bind it to one charged redacted attempt ledger row.
    A bounded internal direct-CSV orchestrator adds deterministic sequential
    admission, continue-on-error execution, exact all-distribution status
    reconciliation, compact successful results, and a strict no-network dry
    run. An internal single-page OGC API Features handler now reuses one held
    all-handler reservation, rechecks its native symbol immediately before
    invocation, performs one DNS-pinned request, and binds strict GeoJSON-to-sf
    normalization to the charged attempt. A cross-handler orchestrator now
    schedules direct CSV, WQP Result, EDR position, current USGS continuous
    and daily data, and OGC
    requests in one global order with shared admission limits, isolated
    failures, compact evidence, and exact all-distribution status. The EDR
    slice binds one CRS84
    point, parameter, UTC interval, and CoverageJSON response to package-owned
    transport and strict offline normalization. The USGS slices
    capability-check dataRetrieval, perform one package-owned current Water
    Data API request for exact site, parameter, time, and daily-statistic facts,
    and preserve string-valued measurements through strict GeoJSON
    normalization. Public bounded fetch planning and sequential execution now
    return a validated supported-subset result with exact all-distribution
    status, handler-native payloads, and retained execution provenance. Public
    offline harmonization now normalizes strict EDR, current USGS, and
    catalog-aligned single-characteristic WQP time-series payloads under
    reviewed fixed-offset timezone, target-unit, and directed affine-rule
    contracts. Explicit distribution-scoped mappings admit strict
    single-variable UTC direct-CSV tables and OGC API Features properties
    without field-name inference. Feature identifiers and geometry never
    supply observation semantics. The exact fetched object and native resources
    remain preserved.
    Public bounded catalog construction supports one graph page, explicit PID
    profiles, or caller-supplied local JSON-LD while retaining problems,
    request evidence, and completeness. Automatic graph discovery remains
    upstream-dependent; provider variants outside the frozen subset,
    pagination, broader semantic alignment, replay, and optional package
    profiles remain under development.
